  "notes": [
   "Fujichrome Velvia 50 Professional [RVP 50]. Traced from the English copy (fuji-velvia-50.pdf, page 8), whose scan has thinner lines than the Japanese copy (fuji-velvia-50-jp.pdf, page 6, document 163AR0128B 17.05); the plots are the same drawings.",
   "Date taken from the print code KAMIQ-07.3-FP next to the reference number in the footer (no other date printed). The Japanese copy shows Status A as 'Fuji FAD-30S (Status A equivalent)'.",
   "Daylight exposure, E-6/CR-56, Status A. x axis of curves is log H in lux-seconds as printed. RMS granularity 9: diffuse, 48 um aperture, 1.0 above minimum density.",
   "curves: r is solid, g dash-dot, b dashed. The three curves lie within about 0.03-0.08 of each other from log H -1.6 to 0.3; g and b cannot be told apart below about log H -1.1 (they share one ink band, traced alike). r runs below g and b on the shoulder, joins them at log H about -1.6 (D about 3.0) and runs above them (by up to 0.08) through the steep part. Above D 3.3 the shoulders are read from the dash-dot (g, about 3.8) and dashed (b, about 3.7) curves; r plateaus at 3.34.",
   "curves end at log H 1.1 (D-min plateau about 0.16 for r, 0.14 for g and b).",
   "sensitivity: y is relative log sensitivity as printed (sensitivity = reciprocal of the exposure in J/cm2 giving density 1.0 above D-min). Each layer's curve ends in a near-vertical drop (blue ends about 495 nm at -0.8, green about 593 nm at -0.8, red starts about 578 nm at -0.8); the tool keeps one y per x, so the last few nm of those drops are cut or approximated.",
   "dyes: spectral diffuse density of the separated dyes (maximum 1.0 each) as printed; the curves start at 380 nm. Yellow falls into the base line (about 0.01) from about 595 nm and is not traced beyond 590 nm. Cyan and magenta cross near 410 nm (both about 0.12) and near 590 nm (about 0.5); yellow and magenta cross near 497 nm."
